WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.890
- I just moved here to
New York and I love it.

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:02.723
And you're from here,

00:00:02.723 --> 00:00:03.556
Hell's Kitchen, right?
- Yeah.

00:00:03.556 --> 00:00:04.560
I'm just down the street,

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:05.787
can you believe it?
- Yeah.

00:00:05.787 --> 00:00:07.933
Did you enjoy this city at all growing up?

00:00:07.933 --> 00:00:09.143
Do you like the energy?
- No, I didn't

00:00:09.143 --> 00:00:11.910
because it was very rough back then.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:12.810
Back then they actually,

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:15.210
it was called Hell's Kitchen for a reason.

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.340
It's because at that time
a lot of different people

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.260
lived in the neighborhood
and they would cook,

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.750
because it's so hot they
would cook on the balconies.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.660
So it smelled like hell's
kitchen 'cause you had cabbage

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.010
with beans.
- Is that how it got the name?

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:27.033
- Yeah, that's how the name

00:00:27.033 --> 00:00:28.500
came, yeah, yeah.
- I didn't know that.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.720
Oh, you learn something new every day.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:31.553
- Now you know.

00:00:31.553 --> 00:00:33.270
- You're just bestowing
knowledge left and right.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:34.103
I like it.
- Thank you.

00:00:34.103 --> 00:00:35.160
- Well, in the documentary,

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:36.630
like you talk about pain and regret,

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:37.860
and that's like what the
character is going through.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.330
But a lot of people relate to that.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:42.840
So how do you deal with
pain or regret in your life?

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.210
- Well, you just have to
say it's going to be there.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:46.980
It's never going to go away.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:47.813
- [Kelly] Inevitable, yeah.

00:00:47.813 --> 00:00:50.920
- And I think you just
understand, you have to accept it.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:53.790
It's part of your history,
part of your culture,

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:57.851
and, okay, I know it's gonna
arrive but I'm prepared for it

00:00:57.851 --> 00:00:59.730
and it will pass.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:01.170
It's like a bad storm.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:02.003
- Yeah.
- It's just,

00:01:02.003 --> 00:01:04.380
but I think people become
disillusioned when they,

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:08.400
oh well, I sought medical
help, I've seen a psychiatrist,

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.573
and they think their
history's going to vanish.

00:01:11.573 --> 00:01:13.020
- No.
- And it doesn't, no.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:17.130
But in some ways it can
be a motivating force.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:17.963
- [Kelly] Hell, yeah.

00:01:17.963 --> 00:01:20.130
- You know, even though it
started like as a negative,

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:22.200
it can eventually end up as a positive

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.740
if you use it instead of internalizing it.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:26.850
- Use it for fuel

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:28.620
for your fire, yeah.
- Yeah, I think so, yeah.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:31.590
But like what you do,
you entertain people.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.210
And when you're being entertained

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:35.940
is when you kinda forget your problems

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.217
for two hours, three hours,
you know what I mean?

00:01:37.217 --> 00:01:39.002
- Why do you think I have nine jobs?

00:01:39.002 --> 00:01:40.847
(Kelly, Sylvester, and audience laugh)

00:01:40.847 --> 00:01:42.442
- Is that one reason?
- I'm like, busy, busy bee.

00:01:42.442 --> 00:01:43.440
Yeah.
- [Sylvester] That's funny.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:44.730
- I do like being busy though,

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:46.260
and I think that is a
thing from childhood.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.120
Like, I'm like, okay, I
must be productive in it.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.300
Not that it necessarily keeps it at bay,

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.070
but I think it gives you a break

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:54.660
to maybe get some perspective

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:56.160
and listen to other people.
- Oh, absolutely.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:57.705
There's no question.

00:01:57.705 --> 00:02:00.450
I think, yeah, the more you
keep yourself motivated,

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:01.320
the better off it is.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.890
- Yeah, I heard that you said
"Rocky" couldn't have existed

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:08.040
without Carl Weathers, right?
- No, you know?

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:09.854
First of all, Rocky the, oh I know.

00:02:09.854 --> 00:02:12.151
- [Kelly] Look at that
photo, it's so iconic, wow.

00:02:12.151 --> 00:02:12.984
- [Sylvester] Yeah?
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:12.984 --> 00:02:14.700
- Yeah, he was amazing.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:17.490
I couldn't find anyone to play the part.

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:19.320
No one had all the tools.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.680
Either their voice was too
high or they weren't big enough

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:23.790
or they weren't athletic enough.

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:27.630
It's really hard to find
everything in a person.

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:30.300
So it was about eight o'clock at night

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.390
and the guy I wanted, (chuckles) he quit

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:36.570
because he wants to go in
a bicycle race competition.

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:37.860
I went "Really?"

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.200
So obviously he was not a serious actor.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:41.880
- Yeah
- He was a physical type.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:45.090
So I'm sitting there and
at 8:30 this fella comes in

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:48.720
and he's mad, like, "What
am I here for, I mean."

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.820
'Cause no one gets it, like,
you don't have an audition

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:53.646
at 8:30 at night, you're like
at the bottom of the barrel,

00:02:53.646 --> 00:02:54.841
you know what I mean?
- Oh.

00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:55.897
- You know what I mean it's like-

00:02:55.897 --> 00:02:57.348
- I didn't know that.
- Noon, lunch, okay.

00:02:57.348 --> 00:02:58.410
- It's like 8:30. .

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:00.390
So he comes in, they
pulled him off a plane

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:01.830
going to Oakland.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:03.877
So he is coming in and he's fired up,

00:03:03.877 --> 00:03:05.490
"All right, what do I do?"

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:06.840
I go, voice, good.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:07.920
Voice is good.
(audience laughs)

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.300
Good, check that.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:10.890
He goes, and he takes his jacket off,

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.075
huh, shoulders forever.
(audience laughs)

00:03:13.075 --> 00:03:13.908
- Check.

00:03:13.908 --> 00:03:17.970
- Check, and then he looked,
I went, good looking.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.550
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- Nice, I didn't expect that,

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:21.780
you know, 'cause he is a boxer.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:22.830
- [Kelly] Tough.
- And then we start,

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:24.827
and tough, then we started to
get up and I'm telling you,

00:03:24.827 --> 00:03:26.670
and he goes, pow, pow, pow.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:28.587
I go damn.

00:03:28.587 --> 00:03:29.420
(audience and Kelly chuckles)

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:31.222
It's like a woodpecker hit me.

00:03:31.222 --> 00:03:32.055
- [Kelly] Oh my god.

00:03:32.055 --> 00:03:34.053
- Check, and then he goes,

00:03:34.927 --> 00:03:39.600
"I would've done better if you
at least hired a real actor,

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.850
instead of, what do
you, what's this guy do,

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:43.117
work in the office?"

00:03:43.117 --> 00:03:44.460
"No, he's Rocky."

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:47.403
He went, "All right, how you doing Rocky?"

00:03:47.403 --> 00:03:48.526
(Kelly and audience laughs)

00:03:48.526 --> 00:03:50.011
He's like, and I go-

00:03:50.011 --> 00:03:52.113
- This guy.
- Arrogant, got it.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.410
Which you needed, you know what I mean?

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:56.243
I wanted that.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:56.243 --> 00:03:58.860
- I wanted him to have
this like Muhammad Ali.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:01.080
He had it all, he had charm,
he had this, he had heart,

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:01.913
he had soul.

00:04:01.913 --> 00:04:04.260
So I was stunned when it happened.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:05.280
I was like crushed.

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:06.990
I didn't think he would ever die.

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:08.590
You know, he's like superman.

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:10.080
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:13.530
So I look back now with
fond, fond, fond memories

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:14.717
'cause it's never gonna happen.

00:04:14.717 --> 00:04:16.500
You know when have you
have those seminal moments,

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:17.333
those crossroads?

00:04:17.333 --> 00:04:18.840
It's like
- Yep. A certain chemistry

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:19.950
on certain things, yeah.

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:22.020
- It's impossible to duplicate.

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:22.915
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- You know,

00:04:22.915 --> 00:04:24.240
it's like if they take a group

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:25.740
and let's say the Rolling Stones, right?

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:27.414
You take Mick Jagger out, it's over.

00:04:27.414 --> 00:04:28.247
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:28.247 --> 00:04:29.820
- You take Mick Jagger as a solo,

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:30.990
I don't think it works either.

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:35.700
There's something about that
combination that was so unique

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.470
that you can't mess with it.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:38.370
- Yeah.

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:40.380
- And I think that happened with Rocky.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:43.500
Everybody in it was like sort
of born to play the part.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:44.430
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:45.660
I love it, and when it
works out like that,

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:46.650
that's why it's so successful.

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:48.065
- Yeah, it is, it is.

00:04:48.065 --> 00:04:49.169
(audience cheering)

00:04:49.169 --> 00:04:51.172
- His Netflix documentary is called "Sly"

00:04:51.172 --> 00:04:52.923
and the new season of
his Paramount+ series,

00:04:52.923 --> 00:04:55.830
"The Family Stallone"
starts February 21st.

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:57.540
The show also stars his family.

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:00.060
So everybody let's welcome them right now.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:03.990
Please say hello to Scarlet,
Sistine and Sophia Stallone

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:06.315
and their mama Jennifer Flavin Stallone.

00:05:06.315 --> 00:05:07.148
(audience cheering)

00:05:07.148 --> 00:05:10.815
(upbeat instrumental music)

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:32.463
Oh my gosh, welcome to the show.

00:05:32.463 --> 00:05:35.035
It's like no one unattractive
is allowed in your family.

00:05:35.035 --> 00:05:35.936
(family laughs)

00:05:35.936 --> 00:05:38.117
Like the whole damn family is attractive.

00:05:38.117 --> 00:05:39.420
- Thank you.

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:40.363
- Makes my life very hard.

00:05:40.363 --> 00:05:41.880
- Beautiful, yes.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:43.230
Well, your life was already hard.

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:45.330
I'm looking at four women
that you had in your house

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:46.830
for many years.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:47.820
That couldn't have been easy.

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:51.210
But speaking of like home life,
y'all just moved to Florida?

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:52.110
- Yes.
- Yeah.

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:53.359
- Oh, we love it.

00:05:53.359 --> 00:05:54.192
I love it.
- Why the move?

00:05:54.192 --> 00:05:57.660
- Because our older two
daughters moved to New York

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:00.450
and then our younger daughter
goes to college in Florida.

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:01.860
So we thought...

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.090
- They follow us everywhere.
- I love it.

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:05.220
- We couldn't, you know,
it was such a long flight.

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:06.270
So we just thought

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:07.560
- [Kelly] It is
- we can move a little closer.

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:08.557
- Yeah, I didn't really realize that.

00:06:08.557 --> 00:06:11.670
So I have to go to LA for
things and I'm like, oh my gosh.

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:13.588
- It's a five and a half
hour, six hour flight.

00:06:13.588 --> 00:06:15.000
- [Kelly] I'm not 20 anymore.
- I know.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:15.833
- I was like, what?

00:06:15.833 --> 00:06:19.020
What do you, do you like when
they come visit in New York,

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:19.853
do you like?

00:06:19.853 --> 00:06:20.774
- No.

00:06:20.774 --> 00:06:22.860
(audience laughs)

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:25.140
- I'm gonna be honest,
I'm gonna be honest.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:25.973
- She said no.

00:06:25.973 --> 00:06:27.220
She goes, yeah, no.

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:28.053
- Don't be vague.

00:06:28.053 --> 00:06:29.910
- I don't wanna lie, I don't
wanna lie on your show.

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:30.750
I don't wanna lie.

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:33.060
My mom, love her, she's
OCD with cleanliness

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:34.740
and my dad tries to fix things.

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.560
For example, we had a
lamp with no light bulb.

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:40.170
So we unscrewed another
lamp to put it in this one.

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:41.340
And then that one had no light bulb.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:42.173
So he tries to help.

00:06:42.173 --> 00:06:43.950
- Well wait, did you
not use the other one?

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:45.009
Is that why?

00:06:45.009 --> 00:06:46.227
- (group exclaiming) we used all of them.

00:06:46.227 --> 00:06:47.850
- But he makes things a
little more difficult.

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:48.810
- [Sylvester] Excuse me.
- [Kelly] Okay.

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:50.980
- It's on you guys, two light bulbs.

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:51.994
It's really easy.

00:06:51.994 --> 00:06:53.040
- [Kelly] . Don't buy more light bulbs

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:54.151
that's what he's thinking.

00:06:54.151 --> 00:06:56.700
All right, so you live
with a lot of women, man.

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:58.290
So like did you have like a man cave?

00:06:58.290 --> 00:06:59.430
- Lucky guy, lucky guy.

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:00.720
- [Kelly] Lucky guy, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:03.510
- But did you have a,
just a corner for you?

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:05.940
- I did, I did for about 10 minutes

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:07.593
and then it became.
- [Jennifer] Oh please.

00:07:07.593 --> 00:07:08.766
(imitating playing a violin)

00:07:08.766 --> 00:07:10.183
- The bear cave.

00:07:10.183 --> 00:07:12.930
- Please, you guys, he had his man cave.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.210
He had a big office where he'd write,

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:18.193
he had another man cave where
he'd sit and watch boxing.

00:07:18.193 --> 00:07:19.080
- No, I didn't, it's a she cave now.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:21.090
- He took over the entire garage

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:22.860
and made it an art studio for him.

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:24.600
So our cars were in the front yard

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:26.220
and he would just like
paint in the garage.

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:27.519
Trust me, he was fine.

00:07:27.519 --> 00:07:28.393
- Oh my God.
- He was fine.

00:07:28.393 --> 00:07:30.028
- They are such liars, it's unbelievable.

00:07:30.028 --> 00:07:32.174
(crowd and Kelly laughing)

00:07:32.174 --> 00:07:33.885
- This is the best television.

00:07:33.885 --> 00:07:35.223
Your show is amazing.

00:07:35.223 --> 00:07:37.070
- Oh my God, Pinocchios all of you.

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:37.903
- Pinocchio.

00:07:37.903 --> 00:07:39.210
Oh my god, I love it.

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:41.070
I love cut to us having footage of like

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:42.060
exactly what they're saying.

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:43.034
- Yeah.

00:07:43.034 --> 00:07:45.240
- So wait, you have a very athletic dad.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:46.675
So did he like play sports?

00:07:46.675 --> 00:07:47.720
- Oh.
- Oh yeah.

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:51.180
- We did every sport in high
school, every single one.

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:52.800
- Sistine broke the record for shot put.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:55.260
- My dad thought before
school, and this is true,

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:58.050
every morning for about four
years, he'd wake us up early.

00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:01.080
We'd have to do pushups,
sit-ups, swing a golf club,

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:02.760
throw a shot put, I
don't know why shot put.

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:03.593
That was

00:08:03.593 --> 00:08:04.426
- [Sophia] Yeah that was a weird one.

00:08:04.426 --> 00:08:06.150
- He said the only two things
he wanted us to do in life

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:07.470
was know how to swing a golf club

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:08.700
and know how to throw a shot put.

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:10.560
And I'm not sure what
the second one was about.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:12.283
We were top shot putters in our grade.

00:08:12.283 --> 00:08:13.336
I'm not sure why.

00:08:13.336 --> 00:08:15.360
- You guys can throw a punch too.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:17.100
- Yeah.
- Yeah, they can hit hard.

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:18.960
- Now that's a good thing to teach them.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:20.190
You might need that one day.

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:21.023
- Yeah, never know.

00:08:21.023 --> 00:08:22.361
- Not every guy's the right guy.

00:08:22.361 --> 00:08:25.230
So did you ever give him any like,

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:28.530
mottoes to like live by, any
advice especially with dudes?

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:30.060
- Oh, well yes, actually I did.

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:31.990
I said think like a man.

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:33.450
- [Kelly] Okay.
- And act like a woman

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:34.283
and you'll figure it all out.

00:08:34.283 --> 00:08:36.180
- [Kelly] Okay.
- You know, because we're not

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:37.830
that complicated.
- [Kelly] Okay.

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:40.590
- I mean, no, all you seriously, we just,

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:43.590
my wife makes me watch the
Housewives of everywhere.

